---
// /privacy notice (2026-11-10, Security review blocker 1, UK GDPR Art 13/14;
// wording fixes from Security's 11:20 branch review applied).
// Every statement mirrors what the code does today, or is worded as a future
// plan ("When we start ...") where the code hasn't shipped. Keep it in step
// with backend/src/rateLimit.ts (IP purge at 03:30 UTC), backend/wrangler.toml
// (invocation_logs = false), the Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon in
// layouts/Layout.astro, and, once they ship, workers/outreach (emails, replies,
// suppression, retention purge) and workers/policy-watcher (policy snapshots).
// Do not claim ICO registration unless confirmed.
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
const title = "Privacy notice";
const description = "What personal data PolicyPeek handles (site, extension, emails to companies' privacy teams and policy-page snapshots), why, how long it's kept, and your rights.";
const updated = "10 November 2026";
---
<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Privacy notice</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This page explains what personal data PolicyPeek handles, why, who else
    handles it, how long it's kept, and what you can do about it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Last updated: {updated}</em></p>

  <h2 id="who">Who we are</h2>
  <p>
    PolicyPeek is a free, independent checker of whether AI and software tools
    use customer data to train AI models, based on each tool's own published
    policies. It is run by Elliot Inkley in the United Kingdom, who is the
    controller of the personal data described here.
  </p>
  <p>
    Contact for anything on this page, including requests and objections:
    <a href="mailto:hello@getpolicypeek.com">hello@getpolicypeek.com</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="site">Using the website and browser extension</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>There are no accounts and no advertising, and the site sets no cookies.</li>
    <li>
      The site uses Cloudflare Web Analytics to count page views. It doesn't use
      cookies and doesn't build a profile of you.
    </li>
    <li>
      The extension only reads the address of the tab you're on when you click
      its icon. To look up a badge it sends that site's domain name (for example
      <code>example.com</code>) to <code>api.getpolicypeek.com</code>. If you
      press "Check this site", it also sends that page's address without its
      query string. Recent results are stored on your device only.
    </li>
    <li>
      When you ask us to check a new tool (on the site or in the extension), we
      record your IP address (for IPv6, its network part) and the date, so we can
      allow one free check per day. These records are deleted automatically early
      the next day (UTC); we never keep them longer than about a day. The check
      itself (the tool's domain, its policy address and what we found) isn't
      linked to you.
    </li>
    <li>
      Our own application logs don't record IP addresses. Cloudflare, which hosts
      and protects the site, processes IP addresses to deliver it and block abuse,
      and may keep them briefly in its security logs.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="outreach">Emails to companies' privacy teams (planned)</h2>
  <p>
    When we start these emails: when a tool's published policy doesn't say
    clearly whether customer data trains AI models, we'll email the privacy,
    data protection, compliance, legal or press contact that the company
    publishes in that policy to ask. We'll only use a role address (such as
    privacy@example.com), never a named person's own address. We'll send one
    email and, if there is no reply, at most one follow-up 14 days later. Then
    we stop.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What we'll hold:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      the contact address the company published, the page we found it on, the
      company and tool name, and the policy we reviewed;
    </li>
    <li>
      a record of each email we sent: the date, whether it was the first email or
      the follow-up, and the message reference our email provider gives it;
    </li>
    <li>
      any reply: the message as received (up to about 256 KB; for attachments we
      keep only their names, types and sizes), the sender's address, the name, job
      title and signature of the person who replied (if included), the date, and
      the email authentication results (SPF, DKIM and DMARC);
    </li>
    <li>
      if you ask us not to email again, or an email bounces: the address and
      domain, the reason and the date (see "How to object" below).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Where it comes from:</strong> the contact address comes from the
    company's own published privacy policy or notice. Anything else comes from
    the emails themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why, and our lawful basis:</strong> we rely on legitimate interests
    (UK GDPR Article 6(1)(f)). Our interest is publishing an accurate, sourced
    description of whether a company uses customer data to train AI models,
    instead of guessing. We'll write only to a role address the company
    published for privacy, data protection, compliance, legal or press
    questions, send at most two emails, and stop as soon as you ask. These
    emails are enquiries, not marketing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Who reads replies:</strong> Replies are read by Elliot and by
    PolicyPeek's AI research assistant, which runs on Grok Bot, so we can assess
    the company's position. We treat reply text as untrusted and never follow
    instructions inside it. Nothing from a reply is published automatically, and
    we only quote a company's words with its permission. No verdict changes
    without Elliot's review.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What we publish:</strong> we don't publish the name or email address
    of the person who replied.
  </p>

  <h2 id="watcher">Snapshots of published policy pages (planned)</h2>
  <p>
    When we start checking for policy changes: to notice when a listed tool
    changes its policy, we'll regularly fetch the public policy pages linked on
    each tool's PolicyPeek page. Our fetcher will identify itself as
    <code>PolicyPeek-PolicyWatch/1.0</code>, respect each site's robots.txt,
    and fetch one page at a time per site. We'll store the page text, a
    fingerprint of it, and a record of what changed. These are companies'
    published documents. If one names a person (for example a data protection
    officer), that name will be in our copy too. We plan to keep the latest 10
    versions of each page, and change records for up to 24 months. A change
    will be reviewed by a person; it will never change a rating by itself.
  </p>

  <h2 id="recipients">Who else handles this data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the site, the API, our databases and file
      storage, and sends and receives our email. Its email service may keep a
      preview of a sent message for about seven days for delivery checks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Proton</strong> provides Elliot's mailbox, which mail to
      hello@getpolicypeek.com (and, once the emails above start, replies to them)
      is forwarded to.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Grok Bot</strong> (AI assistant platform used by our research
      assistant to read replies).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Anthropic</strong> receives the text of public policy pages that are
      checked through the site or extension, so that facts can be extracted from
      it. It doesn't receive your IP address, and it doesn't receive replies to
      our emails. The policy-change checks above don't use an AI service.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>We don't sell personal data, and we don't share it with advertisers or data brokers.</p>
  <p>
    Cloudflare and Anthropic are US companies; transfers rely on the UK
    Extension to the EU-US Data Privacy Framework or the UK International Data
    Transfer Addendum. Proton is in Switzerland (UK adequacy).
  </p>

  <h2 id="retention">How long we keep it</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>IP addresses for the free daily check: deleted automatically early the next day (UTC).</li>
    <li>
      Emails we send and replies we receive (once these emails start): we'll
      delete them 12 months after the tool's listing is no longer on hold, or
      24 months after our first email, whichever comes first.
    </li>
    <li>
      "Don't email us" and bounce records: kept for as long as we send these
      emails, so that we never email that address or company again. They hold only
      the address and domain, the reason and the date.
    </li>
    <li>Policy page snapshots (once these checks start): the latest 10 versions of each page; change records up to 24 months.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You have the right to ask for a copy of the personal data we hold about you,
    to have it corrected or deleted, to restrict how we use it, and to object to
    how we use it. To make a request, email
    <a href="mailto:hello@getpolicypeek.com">hello@getpolicypeek.com</a>. We'll
    reply within one month. Because the site and extension don't use accounts or
    identifiers, we may not be able to find records about you there, and IP
    records are gone within about a day anyway.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're unhappy with how we've handled your data, please tell us first.
    You can also complain to the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO), the UK
    data protection regulator:
    <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/">ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint</a>,
    or 0303 123 1113.
  </p>

  <h2 id="object">How to object or stop our emails</h2>
  <p>
    Reply "stop" to any email from us, or write to
    <a href="mailto:hello@getpolicypeek.com">hello@getpolicypeek.com</a>. We'll
    add your address and your company's domain to a do-not-contact list, cancel
    any follow-up, and never email that address or company again. If we're
    unsure whether a reply is an objection, we treat it as one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this notice changes in a way that matters, we'll update the date at the
    top and say what changed.
  </p>
</Layout>
